## 1. The one-minute mental model

> Audio enters a call session. A session remembers that call's progress. A worker owns model weights and chooses a ready session to compute. A completed compute turn emits text and returns the worker to the scheduler.

The application has **three independent choices**:

| Choice | Example | What it controls |
|---|---|---|
| Model | `models/qwen3-asr-0.6b` | Which learned weights are loaded |
| Runtime | `qwen_stream` | How those weights are executed and session state is retained |
| Transport | `direct` or `network` | Whether PCM reaches the engine through function calls or WebSockets |

Changing transport does not select a different model. Changing language selects a per-call language prompt, not a separately loaded English/Chinese/Indonesian model.

There is one user-facing C++ executable: **`asr-cli`**. `serve` and `load` are subcommands, not separate executables. **`asr-prefix-worker`** is a helper executable used for shared workers when more than one process is requested; its legacy name also serves the resumable runtime. The UI is a separate browser application.

For `qwen_stream`:

- **One worker:** model and inference thread live inside `asr-cli`.
- **Two workers:** `asr-cli` starts two persistent `asr-prefix-worker` processes. Each loads one model context.
- **Two sessions per worker:** four live calls can be admitted. Only one decode turn runs per worker, so at most two worker jobs compute concurrently.
- **Four threads per worker:** numerical kernels can use four compute threads for that one job. A thread is not a call and not a worker process.

```text
service process
  pool/router
    worker 0 process: shared weights + state A + state C -> one ready step at a time
    worker 1 process: shared weights + state B + state D -> one ready step at a time
```

The most useful files to start with are `engine_runtime.cpp`, `prefix_pool.cpp`, `prefix_engine.cpp` and `websocket.cpp`. Their exact locations and responsibilities are catalogued in Section 11.

## 2. Vocabulary, ownership and identities

### 2.1. The objects you will see

| Object | Plain meaning | Lifetime / owner |
|---|---|---|
| `RunConfig` | Validated startup settings | CLI/service, copied into a suite's effective configuration |
| `IASREngine` | A factory that accepts call sessions | Service or CLI load command |
| `IASRSession` | One call's submit/EOF/cancel handle | Browser connection handler or C++ call runner |
| `PrefixProcessPool` | Routes calls to persistent worker processes | Multi-worker shared engine |
| `PrefixProcess` | Parent's handle to one child, its socket and call map | Process pool |
| `PrefixMultiplexEngine` | Shared engine implementation inside a worker | Service for one worker; child for multiple workers |
| `PrefixShared` | Model, locks, inference thread and call states | Shared engine |
| internal `Call` | PCM, language, decoder state, timestamps and result lifecycle | Shared engine's call vector |
| `qwen_resumable_state` | Persistent native encoder/decoder progress | One internal call |
| `IRecognitionSink` | Receiver of transcript events and runtime observations | Outlives the session using it |

A **process** has its own memory space. A **thread** runs inside a process and shares its memory. A **session** is a logical call, not necessarily a thread. A **job** is one selected compute turn. The C++ WAV runner also creates producer threads to emulate simultaneous incoming calls; those are separate from worker compute threads.

Several helper types have similar names in different files:

| Helper | File | What it holds |
|---|---|---|
| internal `Call` | `prefix_engine.cpp` | Actual worker PCM and native resumable state |
| helper `Call : IRecognitionSink` | `apps/asr_prefix_worker/main.cpp` | Child session handle and forwarding callbacks |
| `RemoteCall` | `prefix_pool.cpp` | Parent configuration, sink pointer, snapshot and terminal/reply bookkeeping |
| pool `RemoteSession` | `prefix_pool.cpp` | Parent-side IASRSession proxy to a worker child |
| WebSocket `RemoteSession` | `websocket.cpp` | Client-side IASRSession proxy to a WebSocket listener |

These are layered wrappers for the same logical call, not five different audio calls. Only the worker's internal `Call` owns native decoding state.

### 2.2. IDs are not converted into model numbers

| Identifier | Example shape | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| `recording_id` | `fleurs_en_us_validation_1523_142` | Dataset WAV/reference identity; can be reused by many calls |
| REST `job_id` | `job_<time>_<random>` | Track an asynchronous suite request |
| Suite ID | `load_<time>_<random>` | Group WAV call runs and artifacts |
| Planned call ID | `repeat_0_call_0` | Position/repetition inside a load plan |
| `run_id` in an actual load call | `<suite_id>_<planned_call_id>` | Uniquely identifies one artifact-producing call run |
| actual `call_id` | `<run_id>_call_0` | Session identity used throughout submit, results and routing |
| Browser `run_id` | `ui_<crypto.randomUUID()>` | Identifies an interactive browser run |
| Browser `call_id` | `<browser_run_id>_call_0` | Interactive session identity |
| shared `worker_id` | `prefix_shared_0`, `prefix_shared_1` | Worker assignment, retained despite runtime's newer name |
| process ID | Linux PID | OS process; unrelated to call identity |
| `engine_id` | `qwen_stream_shared` / `qwen_stream_process_pool` | Implementation capability label, not a unique model replica ID |
| `producer_id` | `qwen_prefix_multiplex` | Retained producer label in shared events; not the routing key |
| IPC `request` | Increasing integer per child connection | Match an RPC reply to its request, not identify a call |
| chunk `sequence` | 0, 1, 2… | Check PCM message order inside one call |
| `first_sample` | 0, 3200, 6400… | Audio sample offset, not a timestamp |
| event `sequence` / `revision` | Increasing per call | Recognition-event order and transcript updates |

`new_run_id()` in `repository.cpp` combines a prefix, system-clock value and random values. `frontend/src/stream.ts` uses a UUID. In `load.cpp`, the suite ID is joined with the planned call ID. `run_baseline()` then constructs `<run_id>_call_0`. The simulator happens to run one session in each per-call artifact run; the `_call_0` suffix does not mean only one call can exist in the service.

The shared pool rejects duplicate **active `call_id` values across its workers**, even if callers supply different `run_id` values. The worker child and its engine also check duplicate active IDs. The WebSocket observation feed uses the combined `run_id/call_id` key. Consequently, clients should generate globally unique call IDs for each new logical call, rather than rely on a dataset filename or a model name.

Audio and result events carry identities; they are not routed just by language. Two English calls remain separate even when both use the same WAV and shared weights. A call's `worker_id` is assigned at admission and its affinity remains fixed.

### 2.3. One model family, multiple replicas and private call state

Two workers do not automatically mean two different ASR models. Both use the startup `model.path`. Each process loads a context for the same Qwen weights. Model mappings may share physical file-backed pages within the OS, but C++ pointers/call caches are process-local. There is no distributed shared-memory decoder.

There is no per-call “select model A/model B” router. `make_engine()` chooses the runtime once from startup configuration. `validate_shared_suite()` prevents REST requests from replacing the loaded model, changing workers/threads/slots or changing immutable decoding policy. Supported per-call decode-step controls do not reload weights. Different independently configured model services would be needed for actual multi-model routing.

## 3. Startup: who creates the models and processes

Read `apps/asr_cli/main.cpp` → `apps/asr_cli/service.cpp` → `apps/asr_cli/engine_runtime.cpp`.

1. `main()` parses the subcommand, config path, manifest and `--set` overrides.
2. `resolve_config()` merges YAML/defaults/overrides, validates runtime settings and resolves paths. Model/output paths are resolved against the configuration location; use an absolute path when removing ambiguity.
3. Manifest preparation reads WAVs/references and prepares PCM when the command requires it.
4. `serve` calls `asr::app::run_service()`.
5. `run_service()` calls `make_engine(config)` **before** opening the service listener.
6. `make_engine()` creates `SharedStreamOptions`, with `enabled=true` for `qwen_stream`.
7. With more than one configured worker, the factory constructs `PrefixProcessPool`. With one worker, it directly constructs `PrefixMultiplexEngine`.
8. A multi-worker pool constructs one `PrefixProcess` for each worker index. That constructor calls **`socketpair()` and `posix_spawn()`**. The scheduler does not spawn processes.
9. The child executable receives model path, slots, thread/deadline settings, worker ID and streaming options. It constructs its own `PrefixMultiplexEngine`.
10. `PrefixShared` sets runtime/BLAS thread budgets, calls `qwen_load()`, records model-load time and starts an **inference thread** running `loop()`.
11. The child sends `boot` through local IPC. The parent waits for successful boot before exposing ready worker capacity.
12. The service constructs `ApiService`, its admission gate and public `WebSocketServer`, then prints the loopback address and actual port.

`native_runtime_owner` permits one shared Qwen owner in a process because the pinned native runtime has mutable process-global settings. REST suites reuse the existing shared engine instead of loading another independent shared context into that process.

The native alternative takes a different branch: `SessionManager` + `FactoryWorkerExecutor` + `NativeQwenEngine`. It is not the shared runtime's scheduling path.

## 4. The complete live request flow

This is a real browser/external PCM call to the public `serve` listener.

| Step | Caller / location | What happens |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | `StreamingPanel.tsx` → `wav.ts` | Read/validate the selected PCM WAV and create a streaming controller |
| 2 | `PacedStream` in `stream.ts` | Generate run/call IDs and open `/v1/asr`; observer opens `/v1/observe` |
| 3 | `WebSocketServer::accept_loop()` | Accept a TCP connection and create a handler thread |
| 4 | `WebSocketServer::handle_client()` | Parse HTTP upgrade and the versioned `start` JSON |
| 5 | transport validation | Validate identities, sample rate, chunk limit and optional decode controls |
| 6 | `ServiceAdmissionGate` | Reject interactive admission while an exclusive REST suite owns the service |
| 7 | `engine_.create_session()` | Delegate to the selected shared engine or process pool |
| 8 | pool admission for multiple workers | Reserve a healthy free slot using least-active/round-robin routing |
| 9 | pool `rpc(create)` → child | Child creates a private session state using the same shared weights |
| 10 | server `ready` frame | Return worker ID and one PCM credit to the caller |
| 11 | browser pacing | Send `chunk` metadata followed by a binary little-endian PCM16 frame |
| 12 | server chunk validation | Check sequence, sample offset and payload length, then construct `AudioChunk` |
| 13 | `session->submit()` | In-process: call `PrefixSession::submit`; pool: send an `audio` IPC RPC |
| 14 | worker `PrefixSession::submit()` | Check IDs/order/format, convert PCM16 to floats, append audio, mark readiness and wake inference |
| 15 | worker `loop()` → `select()` | Choose one ready call and prepare a bounded next-step job |
| 16 | `qwen_resumable_step()` | Advance that call's state; copy accumulated text into a C++ event |
| 17 | sink/event delivery | Parent demultiplexes child event by call ID, then `ServerSink` sends it on the call's WebSocket |
| 18 | browser `PacedStream` | Update revisions, transcript and arrival metrics; reject stale observer revisions |
| 19 | browser EOF | Send expected next chunk sequence and total sample count |
| 20 | `finish_input()` | Mark EOF and wait while worker drains remaining quanta; deliver final event then protocol `done` |
| 21 | session destruction | Remove call state and release reserved slot; weights remain loaded |

A chunk acknowledgement confirms `submit()` accepted that chunk. It does **not** mean that audio has already been transcribed. PCM intake can continue while the inference thread computes.

The event sink differs by entry point. A public live WebSocket uses `ServerSink` plus the in-memory observation feed. The C++ WAV runner creates `FileResultRepository` and persists per-call artifacts. Do not assume every external live connection automatically creates the same per-call disk directory as a `load` run.

## 5. Direct and network WAV flows

### 5.1. CLI `load --mode direct`

`main()` constructs the real engine and calls `run_load()`. The runner launches up to `concurrency` producer threads. Each producer picks an input call, constructs a WAV buffer source, clock and file repository, then invokes `run_baseline()`.

`run_baseline()` creates one session, runs `PacedAudioStream`, submits each ready `AudioChunk`, calls `finish_input()`, validates the final state and writes summaries. `RepositorySink` receives events/observations while that is happening.

**Direct skips the WebSocket layer.** With multiple workers, local worker IPC still exists. Direct does not mean all work runs in one process, and does not mean the full WAV is decoded immediately without pacing.

### 5.2. CLI `load --mode network`

The CLI constructs a private `WebSocketServer` around the same actual engine, using an available loopback port. It wraps that port in `WebSocketEngine`. `run_load()` receives the wrapper as its `IASREngine`.

The call runner stays the same. Virtual dispatch changes:

```text
direct:  run_baseline -> actual shared engine create_session/submit
network: run_baseline -> WebSocketEngine -> TCP/WebSocket -> actual shared engine
```

`asr-cli load --mode network` starts its own private listener. It does **not** automatically connect to an already running UI server on port 8081.

### 5.3. REST `/v1/suites` on an existing service

1. The public listener routes the HTTP request to `ApiService::handle()`.
2. The API assigns a `job_id`, checks suite admission and starts a job thread.
3. That thread invokes the callback installed by `run_service()`.
4. The callback resolves request overrides, validates immutable shared settings and prepares `LoadSpec`/`LoadPlan`.
5. It reuses the startup shared engine.
6. Direct mode passes that engine to `run_load()`.
7. Network mode creates a **temporary internal listener** around it and supplies `WebSocketEngine` to `run_load()`.
8. The API returns a job result/suite artifact location when execution finishes.

The public service port accepts the REST request, but the generated network PCM clients connect to the temporary listener's port. That internal listener has no interactive admission gate of its own; the outer gate blocks competing public live sessions while a suite runs. This preserves the exclusive suite boundary while allowing its own internal network clients.

## 6. Call-stack trees

These are **causal call trees**, not one uninterrupted stack. Arrows marked `[thread]`, `[IPC]` or `[socket]` cross execution boundaries; a debugger's `bt` shows only the currently selected thread/process.

### 6.1. Startup

```text
main                                     apps/asr_cli/main.cpp
└─ run_service                           apps/asr_cli/service.cpp
   ├─ make_engine                        apps/asr_cli/engine_runtime.cpp
   │  ├─ [1 worker] PrefixMultiplexEngine
   │  │  └─ PrefixShared
   │  │     ├─ qwen_load
   │  │     └─ [thread] PrefixShared::loop
   │  └─ [multiple workers] PrefixProcessPool
   │     └─ PrefixProcess × worker count
   │        ├─ socketpair
   │        ├─ posix_spawn(asr-prefix-worker)
   │        └─ [child] main
   │           └─ PrefixMultiplexEngine -> PrefixShared -> qwen_load + inference thread
   ├─ ApiService(callbacks)
   └─ WebSocketServer -> [thread] accept_loop -> [thread] handle
```

### 6.2. Direct call, one worker

```text
run_load -> [producer thread]
└─ run_baseline
   ├─ PrefixMultiplexEngine::create_session -> PrefixSession
   ├─ PacedAudioStream::produce_ready / pop
   ├─ PrefixSession::submit -> append floats + notify wake
   │  └─ [inference thread] PrefixShared::loop
   │     ├─ select -> select_prefix_job
   │     ├─ qwen_resumable_create             first decode turn only
   │     ├─ qwen_resumable_step               repeat once per selected quantum
   │     ├─ qwen_resumable_text
   │     └─ RepositorySink::on_event / on_observation
   └─ PrefixSession::finish_input -> wait -> final -> repository.finish
```

### 6.3. One chunk, multiple workers

```text
caller's IASRSession::submit
└─ RemoteSession::submit                    prefix_pool.cpp
   └─ selected PrefixProcess::rpc(audio)
      └─ [Unix socket] child main receives command
         └─ child's calls[call_id].session->submit
            └─ PrefixSession::submit -> wake
               └─ [child inference thread] PrefixShared::loop -> qwen_resumable_step
                  └─ child Call::on_event -> prefix_wire::send(fd 3)
                     └─ [parent reader thread] PrefixProcess::read_loop
                        └─ calls[event.call_id].sink->on_event
```

### 6.4. Network wrapper around the same chunk

```text
run_baseline
└─ WebSocketEngine::create_session
   └─ WebSocket RemoteSession                websocket.cpp; different class from pool RemoteSession
      ├─ JSON chunk header + binary PCM -> [TCP socket]
      └─ server handle -> actual engine session->submit
         └─ previous shared engine / child tree
```

### 6.5. Native numerical work

```text
PrefixShared::loop
└─ qwen_resumable_step                      generated qwen_asr.c
   ├─ audio features / mel frames
   ├─ reuse completed encoder windows or qwen_encode remaining window
   ├─ decoder prefill reuse / qwen_decoder_prefill as needed
   ├─ token generation -> qwen_decoder_forward
   └─ token-history rollback / text update / cursor advance
```

The last tree is conceptual: branches/caching depend on current state. Model layers call CPU kernels/OpenBLAS internally. It is not a promise that every kernel runs on every turn.

## 7. How routing and priority actually work

### 7.1. Two scheduling decisions

| Decision | Responsible code | Inputs | Output |
|---|---|---|---|
| Which worker receives a new call? | `PrefixProcessPool::create_session` | Worker health, reserved-call counts, slot limits, configured policy and rotating tie-break cursor | One worker / affinity assignment |
| Which admitted call runs next? | `PrefixShared::select` + `select_prefix_job` | Available audio, EOF/expiry, busy/terminal/cancelled flags and readiness timestamps | One compute job |

`src/backend/scheduler/scheduler.cpp` defines generic schedulers used by `SessionManager` in the native alternative. **The resumable shared factory returns before constructing that manager.** Its worker selection is implemented in `prefix_pool.cpp`, not through the generic scheduler interface. Opening only `scheduler.cpp` will miss the main shared admission algorithm.

### 7.2. Admission algorithm in the process pool

Under the pool lock, scan workers starting from `tie_break_`:

```text
reject duplicate active call_id
ignore unhealthy worker
ignore worker whose owned-call map has reached its slot limit
score = owned-call count for least_active
score = scan position for round_robin
choose lowest score
reserve call in chosen worker.calls BEFORE IPC create
advance tie_break_ to worker after chosen worker
release pool lock
send child create RPC
if child create fails, remove reservation
return RemoteSession on success
```

The pool's owned-call count includes a session until it is destroyed/dropped. A published final event does not alone erase the parent reservation. This matters when a client rapidly replaces completed calls: lifecycle cleanup and admission timing are separate from token decoding.

Example with two workers/two slots: starting empty, least-active routing can assign A→0, B→1, C→0, D→1. Ties rotate; IDs do not determine priority. A fifth call is rejected while all four slots are reserved. There is no unlimited pending-call admission queue.

### 7.3. Ready-call algorithm inside a worker

A normal call is ready when:

```text
received sample count >= decoded cursor + decode_step_ms × 16
```

The factor 16 is 16,000 samples/s divided by 1,000 ms/s. At 2,000 ms, one quantum is 32,000 samples. EOF allows the final partial quantum even if it is shorter.

`select()` skips busy/cancelled/terminal calls. It checks total deadline and pre-EOF idle deadline. Expired calls form a higher-priority class so they can be retired without expensive inference. Remaining resumable calls all use the same scheduling class; their effective ready key includes step readiness and EOF receipt timestamps. `select_prefix_job()` chooses the oldest key in that class. A rotating scan cursor supplies tie order.

After a turn, the call's cursor/counters are updated. If audio/EOF still makes it ready, `step_ready_ns` is set to decode-finish time. This moves it behind already-waiting calls. There is no customer priority number, language priority or “shortest file first” policy.

For the legacy prefix runtime only, previews and EOF refinement are separate classes and two previews may bypass a waiting EOF. In resumable mode the ready-job `final` class flag is false; that legacy bypass rule does not give EOF a separate privileged class.

### 7.4. Intake and decode backlogs are different

`submit()` appends incoming audio; it does not synchronously decode all of it. The producer can therefore keep delivering while the model falls behind. Delivery lag/PCM queue limits measure transport pacing; the decoder cursor measures recognition progress. After EOF, remaining ready quanta are processed until finalization.

A 500 ms audio quantum taking more than 500 ms of computation can create backlog even with one call. With multiple calls, the worker alternates their quanta and queue waiting increases. Changing a PCM chunk from 200 to 100 ms does not double compute capacity or automatically change the decode step.

## 8. Multiplexing and native state

### 8.1. What is shared and what is saved

`PrefixShared` owns `qwen_ctx_t model` and one inference thread. Each internal `Call` owns a `unique_ptr<qwen_resumable_state>` plus its audio and metadata. The generated state shallow-copies the weight-bearing context but resets/allocates private mutable buffers. Its destructor frees private state without invoking the parent's weight destructor. The parent model outlives every child state.

State includes encoder window cache, decoder KV/prefill capacity, language/tokenizer metadata, raw/stable/emitted token history, audio cursor, chunk index and accumulated text. Caches are associated with the call object, not temporarily indexed only by whichever job happens to run next.

```text
worker turn 1: A(state A, weights W) -> save state A
worker turn 2: B(state B, weights W) -> save state B
worker turn 3: A(state A, weights W) -> continue A
```

This is **time multiplexing**: calls take turns. Tensor batching would put several calls into one numerical operation; this implementation does not do that.

### 8.2. One native turn

`select()` copies cumulative audio up to `min(received, cursor + step)` into `Job.samples`. PCM16 has already been normalized to float. `loop()` lazily creates native state using the call's language/step, invokes `qwen_resumable_step`, copies its text and reads cursor/counters.

Native `step` returns 0 when it needs more audio, 1 after progressing, 2 after finalization, or a negative value on failure/interruption. The generated code retains native window/caching/rollback behavior and limits the call to one iteration per scheduler turn.

Completed encoder windows and unchanged decoder prefills can be reused. The growing partial window can still be recomputed. Thus resumable does not mean zero repeated computation. Cumulative job audio copying and temporary native work remain optimization opportunities.

Optional `refine_final=true` adds a full-audio decode at finalization. The current fast preset uses false. Initial withheld chunks, rollback and token limits influence early-text quality independently of scheduling. The supported resumable API/configuration step range is 500–8,000 ms; the native alternative retains a 1,000 ms minimum. The current streaming-panel dropdown exposes 1,000/2,000/4,000/8,000 ms for this mode, so accepting 500 ms in the backend does not imply it is offered as a UI option.

### 8.3. Thread and lock boundaries

- Producer/connection threads append PCM under the worker mutex.
- The inference thread chooses a job under that mutex, then releases it for numerical work.
- `busy` prevents competing access/destruction of the selected call while the model works.
- Result delivery uses the call's sink after state is updated; terminal completion coordinates with a condition variable.
- EOF/cancel waits release the mutex while waiting. The worker helper handles potentially blocking EOF/cancel RPCs in operation threads so its ingress can still accept other calls' PCM.
- Parent reader threads receive events/replies independently of producer threads waiting for an RPC reply.

Sink/clock lifetimes must cover session callbacks. Session destruction waits until the worker is no longer using its call state, then removes it. Engine destruction joins the inference thread and clears private call states before freeing model weights.

## 9. Sockets and chunk transfer

### 9.1. Two entirely different socket layers

| Layer | Socket type | When used | Payload |
|---|---|---|---|
| Browser/C++ network client → service listener | Loopback TCP (`AF_INET`, `SOCK_STREAM`), HTTP upgrade to WebSocket | Every browser call; simulated `mode=network` | JSON control frames, binary PCM16 frames, JSON results |
| Shared controller → worker child | Local Unix `socketpair(AF_UNIX, SOCK_STREAM)` | More than one shared worker, in either direct or network mode | Length-prefixed JSON commands/replies/events; PCM encoded as JSON integer array |

**Direct + two workers still uses Unix IPC.** **Network + one worker still uses WebSocket TCP**, but does not spawn a shared worker child. These choices are orthogonal.

### 9.2. WebSocket conversation

```text
client -> server: HTTP Upgrade /v1/asr
client -> server: {v:1,type:start,run_id,call_id,language,...}
server -> client: {type:ready,status,worker_id,credits:1}
client -> server: {type:chunk,sequence:0,first_sample:0,sample_count:3200,...}
client -> server: binary frame: 6,400 bytes of little-endian PCM16
server -> client: {type:ack,sequence:0,status,credits:1}
server -> client: {type:event,event:{call_id,kind:partial,text,...}}  whenever ready
... more chunks / acknowledgements / events ...
client -> server: {type:eof,expected_next_sequence,total_samples}
server -> client: final event
server -> client: {type:done,status}
```

JSON and binary frames for a chunk are serialized together by the C++ client sender. The server validates the binary frame length `sample_count × 2`. `first_sample` must match its running sample count. No call ID is repeated in each PCM binary payload: the authenticated/validated start metadata has already associated that connection with one session; each reconstructed `AudioChunk` receives those IDs.

The single-credit protocol bounds outstanding chunk submission. The C++ network `RemoteSession` has a reader thread for acknowledgements/events/observations and waits for the matching acknowledgement in `submit()`. Transcript events can arrive between acknowledgements.

`/v1/observe` is a secondary observation/replay channel. The UI deduplicates revisions because events may be seen through both its main audio socket and observer. Observation buffering is distinct from the model's private state.

### 9.3. Worker IPC conversation

`PrefixProcess` creates the local socket pair, maps the child's end to **file descriptor 3** with spawn file actions, then launches `asr-prefix-worker`. `process_wire.hpp` sends a four-byte length followed by JSON text; the frame limit is 1 MiB. The length is local native representation, not a public cross-platform network format.

```text
parent -> child: {op:create,request:17,run_id:R,call_id:C,language:en,...}
child  -> parent: {type:reply,request:17,status,...}
parent -> child: {op:audio,request:18,call_id:C,sequence:0,first_sample:0,pcm:[...],...}
child  -> parent: {type:reply,request:18,status,...}
child  -> parent: {type:event,call_id:C,run_id:R,kind:...,text:...}
parent -> child: {op:eof,request:19,call_id:C}
child  -> parent: observations, final event, then reply for request 19
parent -> child: {op:drop,request:20,call_id:C}  when remote session is destroyed
```

Worker replies are matched by `request`; asynchronous events are matched by `call_id`. The parent's `calls` map supplies the original sink. PCM is copied/serialized across this boundary, not shared by a raw C++ pointer. There is no HTTP/WebSocket handshake between parent and child.

### 9.4. Why a full slot error can occur after final text

The parent reservation, child call map and worker state are released by session destruction/`drop`, not by the final text alone. Therefore a final event, an EOF RPC acknowledgement and the destruction of the session handle are related but distinct moments. The report's observed network admission failures should be investigated along those boundaries rather than assumed to be model errors.

## 10. The interfaces

Read headers before implementations. `I...` denotes a contract implemented by another class; virtual dispatch selects the actual implementation through a pointer/reference.

| Contract | Main operations | Why it exists |
|---|---|---|
| `IASREngine` | `capabilities()`, `create_session(config,sink,clock)` | Same producer/transport code can use shared, native or network wrapper engines |
| `IASRSession` | `submit(AudioChunk)`, `finish_input()`, `cancel(reason)`, `snapshot()` | Call lifetime and media operations without exposing vendor state |
| `IRecognitionSink` | `on_event`, `on_observation` | Results flow back without model code depending on browser/files |
| `IAudioSource` | `read(max_samples)` | Runner gets PCM without depending on WAV file layout |
| `IResampler` | resample operation | Input normalization separated from WAV parsing |
| `IClock` | monotonic now, sleep-until, domain, UTC | Pacing and measurements share explicit timestamp boundaries |
| `IResultRepository` | begin, append events/audio/records, finish | Artifact publication separated from inference |
| `ISystemSampler` | sample process/host resources | Linux telemetry separated from recognition algorithms |
| `IHttpHandler` | handle request → response | REST API separated from listener/HTTP parsing |
| `IWorkerExecutor` / `IWorkerScheduler` | construct engine / select slot | Native session-manager path, not the shared pool's per-step implementation |

`Status` has an error code/message and boolean success semantics. `Result<T>` pairs status with a value. `AudioChunk`, `SessionConfig`, `SessionSnapshot`, `RecognitionEvent` and `RuntimeObservation` are value structures in `core/types.hpp`, without native Qwen types. `PcmBuffer` is shared immutable PCM16 ownership; submitting a chunk does not transfer a pointer across an OS process boundary.

`unique_ptr` means one owner; destruction releases it. `shared_ptr` means several in-process owners; it does not share address-space objects between processes. `mutex` protects concurrent access. `condition_variable` permits waiting until work/state changes without continuously spinning. RAII means destructors perform cleanup even on exceptions.

