### 12.3. Real WAV simulation without the browser

This command creates its own engine and four total calls, with at most four active producer sessions:

```bash
build/release-cpu/asr-cli load \
  --config configs/qwen_stream_shared.yaml \
  --manifest datasets/manifests/demo_calls.jsonl \
  --set workers.processes=2 \
  --set workers.max_sessions_per_process=2 \
  --calls 4 --concurrency 4 --languages en,id,zh --mode direct
```

Replace `direct` with `network` to insert the private WebSocket listener/client adapter. It still owns its engine, rather than sending a job to your separate UI server.

For an existing server, request the C++ simulator through REST:

```bash
curl -sS -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8081/v1/suites \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{"kind":"load","mode":"direct","calls":4,"concurrency":4,"languages":["en","id","zh"]}'
```

The server must have enough configured slots. `calls` is total work; `concurrency` is active simulated sessions. Neither changes `workers.processes`. An idempotent/overload API response can reject a job before inference; inspect returned status.

Configuration precedence is defaults → YAML → explicit overrides. For this runtime, relevant knobs are `model.decode_step_ms`, `audio.chunk_ms`, `model.threads`, `workers.processes`, `workers.max_sessions_per_process`, `workers.scheduler`, token/withholding/refinement and deadlines. CPU power/thermal settings affect elapsed time but do not select model/session identity.

## 13. Follow a real call in a debugger

### 13.1. Build a separate CPU debug directory

```bash
cmake -S . -B build/debug-cpu -G Ninja \
  -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug \
  -DASR_ENABLE_QWEN_NATIVE=ON \
  -DASR_BUILD_TESTS=OFF
cmake --build build/debug-cpu --target asr-cli asr-prefix-worker asr-native-worker -j 4
```

This enables C/C++ debug information and readable application frames. **The native target explicitly adds `-O3` in `NativeQwen.cmake`, even in a Debug build.** Native locals may therefore be optimized away/inlined. Prefer breakpoints at `qwen_resumable_*` and inspect persistent heap state; full instruction-by-instruction unoptimized native work needs a deliberate debug-only native compile-option change. Do not modify the release target merely to make stepping convenient.

Debugger pauses distort pacing and monotonic deadlines. Use a throwaway output directory and generous validated debug-only lag/time limits; those timings are not benchmark evidence. Do not stop a child at boot for more than its parent's fixed 120-second boot wait.

### 13.2. First walkthrough: one real call, one in-process worker

```bash
gdb --args build/debug-cpu/asr-cli load \
  --config configs/qwen_stream_shared.yaml \
  --manifest datasets/manifests/demo_calls.jsonl \
  --set workers.processes=1 --set workers.max_sessions_per_process=2 \
  --set model.timeout_ms=600000 \
  --set workers.idle_timeout_ms=600000 --set workers.total_timeout_ms=600000 \
  --set audio.max_lag_ms=60000 --set audio.queue_max_ms=60000 \
  --set audio.queue_max_bytes=1920000 --set audio.queue_max_chunks=10000 \
  --set output.directory=../results/debug-call \
  --calls 1 --concurrency 1 --languages en --mode direct
```

Useful GDB commands:

```gdb
set pagination off
set print pretty on
set breakpoint pending on
break asr::app::make_engine
break asr::PrefixMultiplexEngine::create_session
break asr::PrefixShared::select
break qwen_resumable_create
break qwen_resumable_step
run
```

At `make_engine`:

```gdb
print config.runtime
print config.worker_processes
print config.max_sessions_per_process
bt
```

At `create_session`:

```gdb
print config.run_id
print config.call_id
print config.language
print config.decode_step_ms
```

At `select`, step through construction of the `ready` vector. Inspect `calls`, each call's `decoded_samples`, `audio`, `step_ready_ns`, `eof` and `busy`. Do not expect a useful local before its assignment has executed.

At native `qwen_resumable_step`:

```gdb
print n_samples
print eof
print s->audio_cursor
print s->chunk_idx
print s->ctx->stream_chunk_sec
bt
```

The number of samples supplied is cumulative up to this quantum, while `audio_cursor` records already processed progress. An already-existing `s` demonstrates resumption rather than construction of a new decoder every chunk. Two calls should have different state pointers while their parent/shared weight owner is the same worker.

To inspect different threads:

```gdb
info threads
thread apply all bt 6
thread <number>
bt
continue
```

GDB normally stops all threads; media deadlines continue to advance in wall time. Stepping a producer thread will not show the inference loop on the same stack. The sink callback runs in the inference/reader thread, not necessarily the original submitting thread. Avoid scheduler locking when another thread must supply an acknowledgement or release a wait.

### 13.3. Observe actual multiplexing

Use the same debug command with `--calls 2 --concurrency 2`. Both calls use one worker/two slots. Break at `qwen_resumable_step` and compare `s` pointers/cursors across invocations. Break in `PrefixShared::select` to see which call is chosen. A quantum may finish before another call is ready; the sequence need not be a perfectly alternating A,B,A,B at every timestamp.

Watch selection timestamps and readiness, not just arrival order. A call with insufficient audio is skipped until ready. Expiry has precedence; no language or user-visible priority rank is injected by the UI.

### 13.4. Follow a child process

Start the debug `serve` command with two workers. Pool construction starts children before the listener reports ready. For startup tracing:

```gdb
set follow-fork-mode child
set detach-on-fork off
set schedule-multiple on
catch vfork
catch exec
break asr::PrefixProcessPool::create_session
run
info inferiors
```

`posix_spawn` may use a vfork/clone implementation; exact stops vary with libc/GDB. The reliable alternative is to let startup finish, read child PIDs from `/v1/runtime`, and attach a second debugger to a worker:

```bash
curl -sS http://127.0.0.1:8081/v1/runtime
# Use a reported worker process_id, not the coordinating service PID.
gdb -p <worker-process-id>
```

In the worker debugger:

```gdb
break asr::PrefixMultiplexEngine::create_session
break qwen_resumable_step
continue
```

In the controller debugger, observe `PrefixProcessPool::create_session` and `PrefixProcess::rpc`. The model's `qwen_resumable_step` executes in the worker, so a breakpoint set only in the controller cannot show that child's stack. `info inferiors`/`inferior <number>` selects the process; `info threads` selects its thread.

Attaching depends on the host's ptrace permissions. A permission error is an OS debugger-access issue, not an ASR failure. Use a permitted debugger session rather than changing unrelated system policy.

### 13.5. Follow a live WebSocket call

Run debug `serve` with one worker/two slots and connect the browser. Add a breakpoint at `asr::WebSocketServer::handle_client`, or use the file-line landmarks below to stop after the `start` JSON has been parsed.

Inspect `config.run_id`, `config.call_id`, `message`, `sequence`, `first`, `count` and `frame.payload.size()` in their respective scopes. Follow `session->submit` into the shared session. Switch to the inference thread to inspect model progress. At `ServerSink::on_event`, inspect the produced `RecognitionEvent`, then compare it with the browser revision.

The caller's chunk `sequence` and the event `sequence` are separate counters; their numbers need not match. A result's `consumed_samples` is the decoding watermark, while acknowledgements refer to media accepted by `submit`.

