### 13.7. What to inspect when something looks wrong

| Symptom | First locations / evidence |
|---|---|
| A new call is rejected despite final text | pool parent call map, remote session destructor, child `drop`, actual reserved slots |
| PCM acknowledgement is slow | WebSocket reader/ack wait, local IPC `rpc(audio)`, shared mutex; acknowledgement is not model completion |
| No first text yet | audio vs cursor/step readiness; inference turn; native text empty/UTF-8 incomplete; sink delivery |
| EOF delay is large | received samples minus decoded cursor, queued turns, step durations, optional final refinement |
| Wrong language/text in another call | run/call IDs in start/PCM/events; parent call map and private native state pointers |
| Model breakpoints never hit | selected runtime, current process/inferior, generated CPU source rather than pristine vendor source |
| All samples arrived but no successful final | EOF expected count/sequence, finish wait, event status, sink exception |
| Reported CPU exceeds worker thread count | sampled process tree includes controller/producer/socket threads; compute-thread budget is not an OS core cap |

## 14. Reading paths and common mistakes

### Beginner: first hour

1. Read Sections 1–2 and `core/types.hpp`.
2. Read `engine.hpp`: only five call-facing operations need to make sense initially.
3. Read `make_engine()` to see exactly which path your YAML selects.
4. Read `run_baseline()` to understand submit → EOF → result.
5. Read `PrefixSession::submit()` before the model's numerical internals.

### Intermediate: understand concurrency

1. Read `PrefixProcessPool::create_session()` and reservation cleanup.
2. Read `PrefixShared::select()` and `prefix_scheduler.hpp` together.
3. Read `PrefixShared::loop()` and see where the mutex is released.
4. Read worker `main.cpp`, `rpc()` and `read_loop()`; separate requests from events.
5. Trace one call ID across all of those boundaries.

### Advanced: understand compute and measurement

1. Read `prepare_qwen_stream.py` and the generated state definition.
2. Inspect `qwen_resumable_step` and encoder-window/prefill reuse.
3. Inspect decoder prefill/token generation and CPU kernels.
4. Inspect sink timestamp boundaries and `CallMeasurements` summaries.
5. Compare per-call cursor, ready time, job wall duration and input pacing; averages alone cannot explain backlog.

| Misreading | Correct interpretation |
|---|---|
| `--concurrency 4` means four model processes | It limits active simulated calls; worker process count is configured separately |
| Two workers/two slots means only two calls | It permits four sessions, with two worker jobs computing at most |
| Model IDs are derived from call IDs | Model is a startup resource; call IDs route independent state/results |
| The generic `scheduler.cpp` controls resumable priority | Shared worker routing is in the pool; ready-step priority is inside the shared engine |
| Direct means no sockets anywhere | Multi-worker direct mode still uses Unix worker IPC |
| Network load automatically targets the existing UI listener | CLI load owns a private listener; REST suites reuse engine but create an internal network adapter |
| Ack means a transcript was computed | Ack confirms PCM was accepted; decoding runs asynchronously |
| Final text means slot already free | Ownership/destruction/drop releases the slot after finalization |
| Every 100 ms audio chunk produces text | Model readiness follows decode-step/sample boundaries and compute capacity |
| Resumable means every encoder operation is incremental | Completed-window/prefill caches are reused; growing windows can be recomputed |
| More threads guarantee lower latency | Actual performance depends on kernels, contention, clocks, thermal/power configuration and workload |

The main source-reading question is always: **which object owns this call now, which thread/process is running, and which ID carries it across the next boundary?** Answering those three questions makes the rest of the code navigable.
